ts only positive root is (-√3+√63)/2=(3√7-√3)/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_10A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_251","cnm_252","cnm_174","cnm_173"],"id":"regular-hexagon-and-completing-square","label":"The inner hexagon and a radius triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"regular-hexagon-and-completing-square":["cnm_266","cnm_221"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_266","cnm_221","cnm_251","cnm_252","cnm_174","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 4+3*sqrt(2)","reason":"Place AB horizontally and form the octagon by cutting corners from a square. Each cut has legs sqrt(2), so DG is horizontal with length 2+2*sqrt(2), and its distance from A is 2+sqrt(2). The triangle area is (2+2*sqrt(2))*(2+sqrt(2))/2=4+3*sqrt(2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_10B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_180","cnm_138","cnm_112","cnm_173"],"id":"enclosing-square","label":"Find the base and height using a square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"enclosing-square":["cnm_p148","cnm_182","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_182","cnm_174","cnm_180","cnm_138","cnm_112","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (5/9)","reason":"Projections, or the cosine law with ∠ADE=135°, give AE²=s²+r²+√2sr. Hence AF²=s²+√2sr=9+5√2. Since r and s are rational and √2 is irrational, s²=9 and sr=5, so r/s=5/9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_077","cnm_260","cnm_075"],"id":"diagonals-and-irrationality","label":"Use the square's diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonals-and-irrationality":["cnm_191","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_173","cnm_077","cnm_260","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 5√2-7","reason":"The apex is distance 1 from each base vertex, so the pyramid height is H=√(1-(√2/2)²)=1/√2. If the cube side is s, its upper face is the square section at height s, whose side is 1-s/H. Thus s=1-√2s, giving s=√2-1 and volume s³=5√2-7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p160","cnm_133","cnm_173"],"id":"solution:similar-pyramid-sections:244bd9ebe734","label":"Use a horizontal square section","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-pyramid-sections":["cnm_174","cnm_272","cnm_042","cnm_p163"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_272","cnm_042","cnm_p163","cnm_p160","cnm_133","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, √6-√2","reason":"Take l as y=0 and Q=(0,2). Since PQ=3 and QR=5, the horizontal separations are √(3²-1²)=2√2 and √(5²-1²)=2√6. Thus P=(-2√2,1) and R=(2√6,3). The triangle area is half the absolute determinant of P-Q and R-Q, giving (2√6-2√2)/2=√6-√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_173"],"id":"midpoint-horizontal-base","label":"Use the midpoint of PR","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-horizontal-base":["cnm_h181","cnm_p148","cnm_084","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_p148","cnm_084","cnm_p150","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: FI²=8−4√2","reason":"The square has area 4, so AB=2, and AE²/2=1 gives AE=√2. Extend EH to meet BC below B at K. Then BK=BE=2−√2, and FIK is right isosceles. Since [FIK]=[BFIE]+[BEK], FI²/2=1+(2−√2)²/2. Therefore FI²=8−4√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p089","cnm_191","cnm_173"],"id":"solution:external-isosceles-triangle:2a6e3f41aee6","label":"Complete the quadrilateral to a right isosceles triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"external-isosceles-triangle":["cnm_p150","cnm_p148","cnm_137","cnm_138"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_p148","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_p089","cnm_191","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: (16+12*sqrt(2)):1","reason":"Centers of circles of radii R and r are (R,R) and (r,r), so sqrt(2)(R-r) = R+r. Hence q = r/R = 3-2*sqrt(2). The requested ratio is (1-q^2)/q^2 = 16+12*sqrt(2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1971_AMC_12_Q35","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_p192","cnm_173"],"id":"tangency-and-geometric-series","label":"Find the radius ratio, then sum the areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangency-and-geometric-series":["cnm_h181","cnm_h207","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_h207","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_082","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_p192","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, (10-7√2)/3","reason":"Let each cutback along a cube edge be t. Octagon sides satisfy 1-2t=√2t, giving t=1/(2+√2). Each tetrahedron has volume t^3/6, so the total is 8t^3/6=(10-7√2)/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_180","cnm_p148","cnm_p160","cnm_138"],"id":"regular-octagon-cutbacks","label":"Find the cutback from one face","methodDirectNodeIds":{"regular-octagon-cutbacks":["cnm_h111","cnm_181","cnm_174","cnm_173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h111","cnm_181","cnm_174","cnm_173","cnm_180","cnm_p148","cnm_p160","cnm_138"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 5√2 - 7","reason":"The pyramid height is h = √(1 - 1/2) = 1/√2. If the cube side is s, its top section has side 1 - s/h, so s = 1 - √2s and s = √2 - 1. Therefore its volume is (√2 - 1)³ = 5√2 - 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_10B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p160","cnm_133","cnm_174","cnm_h119","cnm_173"],"id":"parallel-square-sections","label":"Match the cube's top face to a square section","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-square-sections":["cnm_p163","cnm_272","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p163","cnm_272","cnm_042","cnm_p160","cnm_133","cnm_174","cnm_h119","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 8√2","reason":"P(z)=(z^4-1)(z^4+7+4√3). Its roots are (±1,0), (0,±1), and (±a,±a), where a=(1+√3)/2. The minimum pairwise distance is √2, with a²+(a-1)²=2. Ordering the roots by angle produces eight edges of that length, attaining 8√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_173"],"id":"solution:cartesian-distance-bound:d495bc8865f8","label":"Locate the roots and bound every edge","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cartesian-distance-bound":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","cnm_h173","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","cnm_h173","cnm_152","cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 4π√3","reason":"By the displayed symmetry, the large circle is centered at the middle unit circle's center. Each row gap is √(2²−1²)=√3, so the top unit-circle center is 2√3 above it. The outer radius is 1+2√3. Thus the shaded area is π(1+2√3)²−13π=4π√3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_173"],"id":"tangent-center-equilateral-triangles","label":"Join the tangent-circle centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-center-equilateral-triangles":["cnm_p192","cnm_p150","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_133"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p192","cnm_p150","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_133","cnm_h181","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: FI²=8−4√2","reason":"The square has area 4, so AB=2, and AE²/2=1 gives AE=√2. Extend EH to meet BC below B at K. Then BK=BE=2−√2, and FIK is right isosceles. Since [FIK]=[BFIE]+[BEK], FI²/2=1+(2−√2)²/2. Therefore FI²=8−4√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p089","cnm_p077","cnm_115","cnm_173"],"id":"solution:external-isosceles-triangle:77f09ec155ee","label":"Complete a right isosceles triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"external-isosceles-triangle":["cnm_p150","cnm_p148","cnm_137","cnm_138"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_p148","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_p089","cnm_p077","cnm_115","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 12√3","reason":"Let each hexagon side be s. A perpendicular projection in a 30° ear triangle gives BF²=(2-√3)s². The total area is (√3/4)(2-√3)s²+3s²/4=(√3/2)s². Equating this to 6√3 gives s²=12, so the perimeter is 6s=12√3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_121","cnm_p148","cnm_173"],"id":"method_1","label":"Separate the three pointed triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"method_1":["cnm_p150","cnm_123","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_123","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_121","cnm_p148","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 2π − π√2 − 1","reason":"AD=BD=√2 and AE, BF have radius 2, so DE=DF=2−√2. The two 45° sectors of radius 2 have total area π and overlap in triangle ABD of area 1. Add the 90° sector EDF and subtract the unit semicircle: π−1+π(2−√2)^2/4−π/2=2π−π√2−1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_137","cnm_116","cnm_p148","cnm_p192","cnm_250","cnm_173"],"id":"combine-three-sectors","label":"Combine sectors and subtract the semicircle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"combine-three-sectors":["cnm_257","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_257","cnm_p150","cnm_174","cnm_137","cnm_116","cnm_p148","cnm_p192","cnm_250","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: (√2 − 1)/2","reason":"Let the center square have side s>0. Since the octagon is regular, each removed corner of its enclosing square is an isosceles right triangle with hypotenuse s and legs t=s/sqrt(2). The enclosing square has side s+2t. The octagon area is (s+2t)^2-4*(t^2/2)=2*(1+sqrt(2))*s^2. The stem explicitly supplies a uniform-area dart model. Thus the center-square probability is s^2/[2*(1+sqrt(2))*s^2]=(sqrt(2)-1)/2, A. Both frozen figures show the same center-square and four-corner geometry; no scale reading is used.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_10B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_180","cnm_138","cnm_p089","cnm_p148","cnm_173"],"id":"enclosing-square","label":"Complete the octagon to a square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"enclosing-square":["math:geometric-probability","cnm_p150","cnm_174","cnm_182"]},"nodeIds":["math:geometric-probability","cnm_p150","cnm_174","cnm_182","cnm_180","cnm_138","cnm_p089","cnm_p148","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2024","reason":"Add a hypothetical 2016th flight. Each complete 12-flight block has displacement (-6,-12-6√3), so 168 blocks give (-1008,-2016-1008√3). Subtracting flight 2016, whose vector is (1008√3,-1008), leaves (-1008(1+√3),-1008(1+√3)). Its length is 1008√2+1008√6, giving 1008+2+1008+6=2024.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h080","cnm_277","cnm_174","cnm_173"],"id":"solution:twelve-leg-vector-blocks:26c491e61648","label":"Pair opposite flights in twelve-flight blocks","methodDirectNodeIds":{"twelve-leg-vector-blocks":["cnm_h081","cnm_h090","cnm_h087","cnm_h056","cnm_h065"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h081","cnm_h090","cnm_h087","cnm_h056","cnm_h065","cnm_h080","cnm_277","cnm_174","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (-4); the area is 16√3 - 23.","reason":"The enclosing square has side 2√3 - 1. Each omitted corner splits into two triangles with combined area (√3 - 1)(2√3 - 3). Thus the polygon's area is (2√3 - 1)² - 4(√3 - 1)(2√3 - 3) = 16√3 - 23, giving m + n + p = 16 + 3 - 23 = -4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_174","cnm_132","cnm_p148","cnm_p089"],"id":"enclosing_square","label":"Subtract the four corner gaps","methodDirectNodeIds":{"enclosing_square":["cnm_p150","cnm_266","cnm_140","cnm_173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_266","cnm_140","cnm_173","cnm_139","cnm_174","cnm_132","cnm_p148","cnm_p089"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 2√3-3 square inches","reason":"The square has side 1. HL congruence of triangles DMC and BNC gives AM=AN=t. Equilateral side equality gives 2t²=1+(1-t)², so t=√3-1. Subtracting the three corner right triangles from the square gives area 1-[t²+2(1-t)]/2=2√3-3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1974_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_121","cnm_p089","cnm_173"],"id":"equal-sides-and-area-subtraction","label":"Equal sides and area subtraction","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-sides-and-area-subtraction":["cnm_139","cnm_p148","cnm_125","cnm_174","cnm_221","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_p148","cnm_125","cnm_174","cnm_221","cnm_p150","cnm_191","cnm_121","cnm_p089","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 552","reason":"For the shown orientation, adjacent centers have separation 8, with components 6 and s−2√3 relative to a triangle side of length s. Thus (s−2√3)²+36=64, giving s=2(√3+√7). The area is (√3/4)s²=10√3+6√7=√300+√252, so a+b=552.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["The shown large tangent configuration is the branch with area10sqrt(3)+6sqrt(7); the other positive branch has area10sqrt(3)-6sqrt(7)<2 and cannot satisfy the given sum of two positive integer square roots."],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_p148","cnm_173","cnm_260","cnm_077"],"id":"solution:tangent-radius-projections:53ea833b6347","label":"Tangent radii and perpendicular projections","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-radius-projections":["cnm_262","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_220","cnm_h181","cnm_p148","cnm_173","cnm_260","cnm_077"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":11,"alignmentIds":["alignment:4efa1ab51ab5d8b8928933c7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (7√3 - 12)","reason":"The minor arcs AB and AC are each 150°, and AD is 120°. Equality DG = AB, together with BG > GA, forces arc AG = 30°, making GC a diameter. Right-triangle angle relations give AF/AB = tan15° = 2 - √3 and AE/AC = √3tan15° = 2√3 - 3. Their product is 7√3 - 12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_173"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1